From e468720dd284ac0ef52a2b1a0a5a5994f99f5f11 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SimplicityGuy <robert@simplicityguy.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 18:18:16 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] feat(audioloader): add startTime/endTime seeking to
 AudioLoader and MonoLoader

Closes the head of #771. EasyLoader has exposed startTime/endTime since forever,
but implements them by decoding the ENTIRE stream from byte 0 and trimming, so
extracting a slice costs O(total duration) no matter how short the slice is.
Analysing a long recording in N windows therefore costs O(N x duration) rather
than O(duration), which is the inefficiency the issue describes.

AudioLoader already owns the demuxer and codec contexts, so the seek belongs
here:

  * openAudioFile() now finishes by calling seekToStartTime(), which issues
    av_seek_frame(..., AVSEEK_FLAG_BACKWARD) at (startTime - 0.5 s) and flushes
    the decoder and the resampler.
  * process() stops and reports FINISHED once endTime has been produced, instead
    of reading on to EOF.
  * copyFFmpegOutput() trims the partial frames at both ends of the slice.

SAMPLE ACCURACY is the part that needed care, and it is why the seek target is
half a second EARLY rather than exact. A compressed stream can only be seeked to
a packet boundary, and the packet it lands on is not independently decodable --
MP3 carries a bit reservoir between frames and every MDCT codec needs the
previous window. So we deliberately land before the target, decode forward
discarding output until the exact requested sample, and only then start
producing. The exact sample is located from the first decoded frame's pts, NOT
assumed to be the packet boundary, which is why setting pkt_timebase (submitted
separately) matters for Ogg/Opus.

Seconds are converted to samples the way Trimmer has always done it -- truncation
-- so that the composites which already expose these parameters can be switched
over to seeking without moving a single sample. The multiplication is done in
double rather than in Real: Trimmer's float32 mantissa cannot represent every
sample index past ~380 s at 44.1 kHz, so its cut points drift off the sample grid
on long files, and there is no reason to reproduce that here.

An inverted range (startTime > endTime) is an error, as it already was; an EMPTY
range (startTime == endTime) is not, and yields no audio, which is also what
Trimmer does today.

Verified against the decode-from-zero-and-trim path on synthetic audio at several
offsets:

  wav / mp3 / flac    bit-identical
  ogg (Opus)          bit-identical, apart from one 2^-24 ULP at one offset
  m4a (AAC)           bit-identical when the encoder did not use PNS

The AAC caveat is a property of the codec, not of this patch: Perceptual Noise
Substitution synthesises noise bands from a PRNG whose state depends on the
whole decode history, so no amount of preroll can reproduce it -- confirmed by
the residual being completely insensitive to preroll length (0.05 s to 10 s) and
vanishing entirely when the file is re-encoded with -aac_pns 0. The affected
bands are synthesised noise by construction.

Failure is soft everywhere it can be: a seek that fails, or a stream with no
usable timestamps, falls back to decoding from the beginning and discarding.
That is exactly today's behaviour -- slow, but correct -- so no format that
works now can break.

MonoLoader forwards both parameters. Note that when it also RESAMPLES, the
resampled output is not bit-identical to a from-zero decode, at either end:
libsamplerate's filter state and phase accumulator depend on how much input it
has already seen, and its last output samples depend on input past the end of the
slice. Measured at -49 dB relative RMS, with BPM and key estimates unchanged.

Signed-off-by: SimplicityGuy <robert@simplicityguy.com>
